{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1919 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "af8d7e85c26f1a9feea18c42e3dbfd17b44bbafdead33e7dfa5fa9cc9773d76d" :edb/db-before-t 1918 :edb/db-after-t 1919 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535231 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:03:51.473Z" 13194139535231 true] [17592186124520 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338910 13194139535231 true] [17592186124520 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338917 13194139535231 true] [17592186124521 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338930 13194139535231 true] [17592186124521 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338937 13194139535231 true] [17592186125008 1017 #edb/ref 17592186123070 13194139535231 false] [17592186125008 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338924 13194139535231 true] [17592186125008 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535231 true] [17592186125008 1100 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = B$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535231 false] [17592186125008 1100 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535231 true] [17592186125008 1145 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-8\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535231 true] [17592186338910 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338911 13194139535231 true] [17592186338910 1089 "q-140040" 13194139535231 true] [17592186338910 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535231 true] [17592186338910 1100 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139535231 true] [17592186338910 1113 #uuid "a0328f15-f31e-587d-938d-d61590167bae" 13194139535231 true] [17592186338910 1145 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -32+36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-2I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139535231 true] [17592186338911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338912 13194139535231 true] [17592186338911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338913 13194139535231 true] [17592186338911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338914 13194139535231 true] [17592186338911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338915 13194139535231 true] [17592186338911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338916 13194139535231 true] [17592186338911 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535231 true] [17592186338911 1041 "selection" 1319413